   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the tax law, in relation to the disposition of a portion of sales taxes collected for hotel occupancy in cities having a population of one million or more",
    "basePrintNo": "S2238",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S2238-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §1261, Tax L",
    "lawSection": "Tax Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S2238\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the tax law, in relation to the\ndisposition of a portion of sales taxes collected for hotel occupancy\nin cities having a population of one million or more\n\nPURPOSE:\n\nTo provide each of the five boroughs of New York City with a modest\nstream of dedicated funding to promote tourism within each individual\nborough.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 provides that either 4 percent of hotel occupancy tax\nrevenue collected in each of New York City's five boroughs or\n$300,000, whichever is less, will be allocated to that borough for the\npurpose of promoting tourism.\n\nSection 2 establishes the effective date.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nWhile New York's economy is still not fully recovered from the traumas\nof the Great Recession, there is no question we are on our way back.\nOne critical reason why is that even as other industries struggle\nrecover, at least one of its major source of jobs and revenue is\nthriving like never before: New York City Tourism. In 2012, the City\nwelcomed a record-breaking 52.7 million visitors despite the\ndevastation wrought by Hurricane Sandy. In 2013, we broke that record\nagain with a staggering 54.3 million visitors. According to a report\nby NYC & Company, tourism supports 350,000 jobs and visitors to our\ncity paid for more than 30 million hotel room nights and poured $39\nbillion directly into the city's economy. Moreover, the city's hotel\noccupancy stands at 87 percent, the highest in the nation. Better yet,\nthere is good reason to believe that tourism remains a growth\nindustry, with much of its potential still untapped, particularly\noutside of Manhattan. In the past seven years, 86 new hotels have been\nbuilt in boroughs other than Manhattan and a record-high percentage of\nnon-Manhattan hotels are under development for the next four years.\n\nWith historic and burgeoning neighborhoods like Arthur Avenue and\nWoodlawn in the Bronx, St. George and Snug Harbor in Staten Island,\nRidgewood, Jackson Heights and Astoria in Queens, and Fort Greene,\nProspect Heights and Bushwick in Brooklyn, not to mention dozens of\nother great neighborhoods across the city, it is clear that the\npotential of the \"outer boroughs\" is near limitless. That is without\neven considering up-and-coming neighborhoods in Manhattan like\nAlphabet City and Harlem.\n\nMayor de Blasio and NYC & Company deserve enormous credit for\nnurturing the tourism boom, and they are doing excellent work\npromoting tourism beyond Manhattan with initiatives like \"Neighborhood\nX Neighborhood,\" which is \"designed to support local businesses and\nencourage tourism in neighborhoods outside traditional tourist\nlocations.\"  However, it is no slight to the extraordinary job they\nhave done to say that no one knows the Bronx like people from the\n\n\nBronx, or that no one knows Queens like the people of Queens, right on\ndown the line. In order best highlight the hidden gems of the five\nboroughs it makes sense to turn at least a small portion of the\npromotional responsibilities to entities that know their home boroughs\nbetter than anyone else.\n\nExperts in tourism promotion view it as an enterprise of concentric\ncircles: with the largest being international tourism and the smallest\nbeing intraborough tourism. NYC & Company has done superlative job\npromoting the wonders of New York City across the globe, and the\nnumber of international visitors has more than doubled over the last\ndecade. They have also had tremendous success in marketing our city to\nvisitors from across the country and within the state itself. Finally,\nwith the recent \"See Your City\" campaign, they have begun a concerted\ncampaign to encourage New Yorkers to explore their own city. This\nlegislation is intended to create a seamless synergy between these\nbroader efforts, and carefully targeted campaigns to promote\noff-the-beaten-path sites and experiences between boroughs and even\nwithin a single borough. It's extremely possible to be a resident of\nWilliamsburg who has never ventured into Sunset Park for Dim Sum or\neaten a piroska while strolling Brighton Avenue. It's far too easy to\nlive in Long Island City without making the trip out to Woodside for\nPanang Curry or Korean Bar-B-Que\n\nThis legislation will help foster the growth and expansion of New York\nCity's tourism industry by turning over a small portion of each\nborough's hotel occupancy tax revenue (the lesser of 4% or $300,000)\nto interborough entities designated by local law and approved by the\nEconomic Development Corporation for the promotion of local\nrestaurants, sights, and attractions. By reallocating resources for\nthis purpose, we can help foster our tourism boom in a long-term and\nsustainable fashion.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2013-2014: S4986-A\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNo revenue will be lost, but a maximum of $1.5 million in tax revenue\nwill be reallocated annually for the purpose of promoting tourism in\nthe five boroughs of New York City. This borough-specific promotion\nshould, in turn, increase the economic impact of tourism.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis act shall take effect on the first of January next succeeding the\ndate on which is shall have become law.\n\n",
